      "abstract": "<jats:title>ABSTRACT</jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Ascl1 is a pioneer transcription factor that drives neuronal fate decisions, yet the structural basis of its activity remains elusive. Besides the basic helix–loop–helix (bHLH) domain which dimerizes with other transcription factors and binds DNA, Ascl1 contains long low-complexity intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs), including a polyA/polyQ tract of unknown function. Here we use single-molecule FRET to map the conformational ensemble of full-length Ascl1 across monomeric, heterodimeric, and DNA-bound states. Monomeric Ascl1 is largely disordered but displays multivalent coupling between the N-terminal IDR and the bHLH domain, with the polyA/polyQ tract opposing bHLH compaction. E12 binding folds the bHLH domain and remodels the N-IDR, increasing dynamics across the N-IDR while suppressing them locally within the polyA/polyQ tract. Deleting the tract weakens nonspecific DNA binding by the Ascl1/E12 heterodimer without disrupting heterodimerization or high-affinity E-box binding, abolishes aggregation in vitro, and increases Ascl1 abundance in HEK293T cells. Thus, the polyA/polyQ tract acts as a regulatory module that promotes nonspecific DNA engagement while imposing a cost in solubility and cellular abundance.</jats:p>",
